; bfsodium KMAC256 : NIST SP 800_185 section 4's KMAC at SHAKE256's rate
;
; NOTE square brackets are brainfuck loops  so comments use braces for counts;
;
; HAND WRITTEN skeleton; keccak/bytepad136 is PASTED TWICE  keccak/rightenc
; once  chacha20/add32 once  and keccak/sponge136 once; This file adds no
; cryptography of its own  which is the point of it;
;
; IO  in:  xof{1}  klen{2} LE  slen{2} LE  mlen{2} LE  olen{2} LE
;          kbuf{128}  sbuf{128}   (265 bytes)  then mlen bytes from the wire
;     out: olen bytes
;
;   KMAC256(K  X  L  S) = cSHAKE256( bytepad( encode_string(K)  136 )
;                       then X then right_encode(L)   L  "KMAC"  S )
;
; and cSHAKE in turn is the sponge with a tape prefix  so the whole of KMAC is
; three pieces of tape and a wire: cSHAKE's own bytepad block over the name
; KMAC and the customization string  then a SECOND bytepad block over the key
; then the message from the wire  then right_encode of the output length;
;
; THE KEY GETS A BLOCK OF ITS OWN AND THAT IS WHERE KMAC's SECURITY LIVES; it
; is bytepad and not concatenation  so the key fills a whole rate and the
; permutation runs over it before a single byte of the message arrives; the
; sponge's prefix buffer has always been two rates deep for this;
;
; XOF IS ONE CELL AND NOT A SECOND FILE; KMACXOF is KMAC with right_encode(0)
; in place of right_encode(L)  and right_encode of nought is what this file's
; own encoder produces when it is handed nought; so the flag does not branch
; around the encoder  it CLEARS ITS INPUT: xof{1} nought is KMAC256 and one
; is KMACXOF256; A rate cannot be a parameter in brainfuck  a padding byte
; can  and so can this;
;
; L IS IN BITS IN THE STANDARD AND IN BYTES ON THIS WIRE; olen is a byte count
; like every other length in this library  and right_encode is handed eight
; times it; eight is three doublings of chacha20/add32  exactly as
; keccak/bytepad136 multiplies a string's length;
;
; KLEN IS AT MOST 128 and SLEN AT MOST 124  which are keccak/bytepad136's
; limits and the size of the buffers here; the routine DOES NOT CHECK THEM;
;
; THE KEY BLOCK IS BYTEPAD OF ONE STRING AND NOT TWO  which is the flag that
; keccak/bytepad136 grew for this file; getting it wrong is not a crash and
; not a contract failure: it appends encode_string of the empty string  which
; is the two bytes 01 00  and every answer is silently wrong; That is how it
; was found  by every vector failing at once while every contract passed;
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x000        pad     the padding byte the sponge is handed  which is 4
;   @0x001:0x002  mlen{2} the message length  little endian
;   @0x003:0x004  olen{2} the output length
;   @0x005:0x006  plen{2} how much prefix the sponge takes  which is two rates
;   @0x007:0x116  the sponge's own prefix staging; the two blocks go here
;   @0x117:0x118  the sponge's suffix length  and
;   @0x119:0x120  its suffix buffer  where right_encode's answer goes
;   @0x000:0x527  keccak/sponge136 pasted at this file's own zero
;   @0x530:0x6f3  keccak/bytepad136's frame  pasted at @0x530  and used TWICE;
;                 its answer is at @0x638 and it is moved out after each run
;   @0x6f8:0x800  everything read  parked above both frames; the cells it
;                 arrives in are inside the sponge's own
;   @0x806:0x817  keccak/rightenc's frame  pasted at @0x806; footprint 0:17
;   @0x81a:0x82d  chacha20/add32's frame  pasted at @0x81a; footprint 0:19
;   @0x82e:0x831  tmp{4}  hands the accumulator back to itself
;   @0x832        dbl     the three doublings still to do
;   @0x833        xofc    the flag  as the clear reads it
;
; THE TWO PASTED FRAMES DO NOT OVERLAP and the bytepad one is REUSED: the
; sponge owns nought to 0x527  bytepad sits above it  and bytepad leaves its
; own frame at nought once its block is carried away  which is what lets one
; paste serve both blocks; there is a contract after each run that says so;

; read the flag  the four lengths  the key and the customization string
,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,
